# Markdown Style

This rule applies to **every `.md` file in the repository** (root README, app/package READMEs, `docs/`, etc.). The intent is to keep our documentation visually consistent and reviewer-friendly. **`.cursor/rules/*.mdc` files are exempt** from these spacing rules — they follow Cursor's own front-matter conventions.

## 1. Spacing and Structure

- One sentence per concept. Prefer short paragraphs over walls of text.
- **One blank line** between every heading and the content below it.
- **One blank line** between every paragraph, list, table, and fenced code block.
- **One blank line** before and after every fenced code block (this includes Mermaid blocks). Prettier enforces this.
- **One blank line** between adjacent sections (`##` siblings) for visual separation.
- Do **not** stack multiple blank lines (Prettier collapses them to one).
- Do **not** place text immediately under an `H1` without a one-line summary first; lead with intent.
- Bullet lists: use `-` (not `*` or `+`); keep one bullet per line; never indent without nesting.
- Tables: surround with one blank line above and below; align pipes for readability when the table is small enough.
- File / symbol references inline as `inline code`. Use the path/file:line convention from `coding-standards.mdc` when pointing to a specific location.

## 2. Headings

- A `.md` document has exactly **one** `H1` (the title).
- Never skip levels (no `H1` followed by `H3`).
- Headings should be sentence case, not title case (`## Quick start`, not `## Quick Start`) — except brand names (`Hyperswarm`, `OpenAPI`).
- Trailing punctuation on headings is forbidden.

## 3. Code Blocks

- Always include a language identifier: `bash`, `ts`, `json`, `mermaid`, `text`, `yaml`, etc.
- For shell commands, use `bash` even on zsh-only snippets.
- Inline expected output as a separate block, labelled with `text` or `json`.
- For multi-step commands, prefer numbered steps with one block per step rather than chaining everything in one block.

## 4. Mermaid Diagrams

A diagram is **required** whenever any of the following are true:

- The document explains a **system architecture** (component layout, data flow, transport split).
- The document describes a **request lifecycle**, **message exchange**, or **protocol** that crosses two or more processes.
- The document describes a **state machine**, **enum-driven workflow**, or **lifecycle** with three or more states.
- Two paragraphs of prose would otherwise be needed to explain something a single diagram can convey at a glance.

When in doubt, prefer a diagram. The reviewer should be able to skim the document and grasp the architecture from diagrams alone.

### 4.1 Diagram type cheat sheet

- **`flowchart TD`** — components, modules, data flow, layered architecture.
- **`sequenceDiagram`** — request/response timing, RPC interactions, streaming protocols. Always add `autonumber`.
- **`stateDiagram-v2`** — lifecycles (job state, key state, connection state).
- **`classDiagram`** — only when documenting public APIs / inheritance; rarely needed.
- **`erDiagram`** — Hyperbee key namespaces or relational mappings.

### 4.2 Mermaid authoring rules

- Wrap every diagram in a fenced code block with the `mermaid` language identifier.
- Keep each diagram under ~30 lines. If a diagram grows beyond that, split it into two.
- Use **short labels** in shapes (≤4 words). Move detail into the prose surrounding the diagram.
- Sequence diagrams: declare every participant up front with a single-letter alias (`participant B as Backend`); use the alias in messages.
- State diagrams: name every transition; never leave an arrow unlabelled.
- Avoid colors and styling — leave defaults so GitHub's renderer stays clean.
- Reference one diagram per section; never embed two consecutive diagrams without prose between them.

### 4.3 Example

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant B as Backend
    participant W as Worker

    C->>B: POST /v1/chat/completions
    B->>W: rpc.request("inference.run")
    W-->>B: InferenceResponseFinal
    B-->>C: 200 ChatCompletion
```

## 5. Document Skeleton

Every README under `apps/*` or `packages/*` should follow this skeleton:

1. `H1` with the package name and one-sentence purpose.
2. **Responsibilities** — bullet list, ≤7 items.
3. **Architecture diagram** — Mermaid `flowchart TD` showing where this package sits relative to its peers, when applicable.
4. **Project structure** — fenced `text` block with the `src/` tree and one-line annotations.
5. **Scripts** — fenced `bash` block listing common commands.
6. **Environment variables** — table or link to `.env.example`.
7. **Related rules** — bullet list linking back to `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` files.

`docs/architecture.md` follows a different (longer) skeleton — see the file itself for the canonical structure.

## 6. Cross-document Conventions

- Always link to other markdown documents using **relative paths** (`./architecture.md`, not `https://...`).
- When citing source code, use the `path/file:line` convention from `coding-standards.mdc`.
- When listing trade-offs, link to the canonical entry in `docs/architecture.md` §4 instead of duplicating prose.
- When listing future improvements, link to `docs/architecture.md` §6 instead of duplicating prose.

## 7. Enforcement

- `pnpm format:check` (Prettier) enforces the spacing rules on every PR via CI.
- The AI assistant **must** add or update Mermaid diagrams whenever it adds a new architectural concept (transport, lifecycle, integration point) per §4.
- The reviewer is allowed to reject documentation PRs that lack a diagram for a system / lifecycle / protocol explanation.
